1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,793
Right now, about thirty-eight trillion bacteria are living in and on your body.

2
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:08,589
That's more than the number of cells that are actually you.

3
00:00:08,589 --> 00:00:11,103
Together, they weigh about two hundred grams.

4
00:00:11,103 --> 00:00:12,835
Roughly the weight of an apple.

5
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:17,018
So what if, in one instant, every single one of them vanished?

6
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:22,486
Here are the rules.

7
00:00:22,486 --> 00:00:26,852
At exactly midnight, every bacterium in and on your body disappears.

8
00:00:26,852 --> 00:00:28,906
Your gut, your skin, your mouth.

9
00:00:28,906 --> 00:00:30,062
All of them, gone.

10
00:00:30,562 --> 00:00:33,962
Viruses and fungi stay, just to keep things simple.

11
00:00:33,962 --> 00:00:36,296
And nothing else about you changes.

12
00:00:36,296 --> 00:00:38,630
Your own cells are completely fine.

13
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:43,812
To make sure the bacteria can't sneak back in, you move into a sterile room.

14
00:00:43,812 --> 00:00:48,803
The air is filtered, and every meal and every glass of water is sterilized first.

15
00:00:49,303 --> 00:00:52,028
Labs have kept animals like this for decades.

16
00:00:52,028 --> 00:00:54,027
They're called germ-free animals.

17
00:00:54,027 --> 00:00:57,359
So we know a surprising amount about what happens next.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,656
But first, how many bacteria are we actually talking about?

19
00:01:03,656 --> 00:01:07,936
For decades, textbooks said they outnumbered your own cells ten to one.

20
00:01:08,436 --> 00:01:10,964
That ratio traces back to a quick,

21
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:13,939
back-of-the-envelope estimate from 1972,

22
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:16,616
by the microbiologist Thomas Luckey.

23
00:01:16,616 --> 00:01:19,739
It got repeated for more than forty years.

24
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:25,505
In 2016, a team led from Israel's Weizmann Institute did a careful recount.

25
00:01:25,505 --> 00:01:26,418
Their answer:

26
00:01:26,418 --> 00:01:32,035
about thirty-eight trillion bacteria, against about thirty trillion human cells.

27
00:01:32,535 --> 00:01:34,322
So it's closer to one to one.

28
00:01:34,322 --> 00:01:36,048
And here's the strange part.

29
00:01:36,048 --> 00:01:40,671
About eighty-four percent of your own cells, by count, are red blood cells.

30
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:44,563
Almost all of those bacteria live in one place:

31
00:01:44,563 --> 00:01:45,272
your colon.

32
00:01:45,272 --> 00:01:49,913
The part packed with bacteria holds only about four hundred milliliters.

33
00:01:49,913 --> 00:01:52,040
A little more than a can of soda.

34
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:55,098
Your skin carries around a trillion more.

35
00:01:55,098 --> 00:01:57,532
And your mouth, despite its reputation,

36
00:01:57,532 --> 00:02:00,651
holds less than one percent as many as your colon.

37
00:02:01,151 --> 00:02:05,028
The two totals are so close that, as the researchers pointed out,

38
00:02:05,028 --> 00:02:09,798
a single trip to the toilet may tip the balance back in favor of your own cells.

39
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:13,064
Midnight.

40
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:14,244
The bacteria vanish.

41
00:02:14,244 --> 00:02:16,426
And at first, you don't feel a thing.

42
00:02:16,926 --> 00:02:18,956
You're about two hundred grams lighter,

43
00:02:18,956 --> 00:02:21,557
and almost all of that is missing from your colon.

44
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:24,277
Your skin looks exactly the same.

45
00:02:24,277 --> 00:02:26,564
Bacteria are far too small to see.

46
00:02:26,564 --> 00:02:29,457
A typical one is about a micrometer across.

47
00:02:29,457 --> 00:02:32,619
Line up around a hundred of them, side by side,

48
00:02:32,619 --> 00:02:36,184
and they'd just about span the width of a human hair.

49
00:02:37,033 --> 00:02:39,703
But you've also lost a gigantic library.

50
00:02:39,703 --> 00:02:46,310
A 2010 study catalogued about three point three million different bacterial genes in the human gut.

51
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:51,181
That's more than a hundred and fifty times as many as in your own genome.

52
00:02:51,681 --> 00:02:53,617
Each of those genes is a tool.

53
00:02:53,617 --> 00:02:56,586
Tools for breaking down food, making vitamins,

54
00:02:56,586 --> 00:03:00,135
and building chemicals your body can't make on its own.

55
00:03:00,135 --> 00:03:02,071
And you just lost all of them.

56
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:04,194
There is one bonus.

57
00:03:04,194 --> 00:03:07,446
Body odor isn't really the smell of sweat itself.

58
00:03:07,446 --> 00:03:09,702
It's mostly made by skin bacteria,

59
00:03:09,702 --> 00:03:14,082
which break down molecules in sweat into pungent sulfur compounds.

60
00:03:14,582 --> 00:03:18,148
Some of them can be smelled at just one part per trillion.

61
00:03:18,148 --> 00:03:21,283
So with no bacteria, your armpits go a lot quieter.

62
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:26,195
By the next day, your gut notices.

63
00:03:26,195 --> 00:03:29,755
Most of the fiber you eat reaches your colon undigested,

64
00:03:29,755 --> 00:03:32,425
because your own body can't break it down.

65
00:03:32,925 --> 00:03:35,296
Normally, bacteria take over there.

66
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:38,886
They ferment that fiber into short-chain fatty acids,

67
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:42,205
with names like acetate, propionate and butyrate.

68
00:03:42,705 --> 00:03:46,421
Butyrate is the key fuel for the cells lining your colon.

69
00:03:46,421 --> 00:03:48,376
Most of your cells burn sugar.

70
00:03:48,376 --> 00:03:50,983
These ones run on food made by bacteria.

71
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:54,101
So now, they're running on empty.

72
00:03:54,101 --> 00:03:55,200
In a 2011 study,

73
00:03:55,200 --> 00:04:00,010
colon cells from germ-free mice were found in an energy-starved state.

74
00:04:00,010 --> 00:04:04,063
They even started digesting parts of themselves to survive.

75
00:04:04,563 --> 00:04:08,775
When the researchers gave those cells butyrate, the energy shortage went away.

76
00:04:09,275 --> 00:04:13,314
Meanwhile, the fiber you eat just passes straight through.

77
00:04:13,314 --> 00:04:14,498
One small upside:

78
00:04:14,498 --> 00:04:17,144
bacterial fermentation also makes gas.

79
00:04:17,144 --> 00:04:18,745
So, a lot less of that.

80
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:24,976
After a week, the missing chemistry starts to matter.

81
00:04:24,976 --> 00:04:30,485
Gut bacteria make vitamin K, which your blood needs to clot, and several B vitamins.

82
00:04:30,985 --> 00:04:36,388
In studies where volunteers ate a diet low in vitamin K, they stayed fine for weeks.

83
00:04:36,388 --> 00:04:39,733
But when they also took a broad-spectrum antibiotic,

84
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:43,528
the level of a key clotting protein in their blood dropped.

85
00:04:44,028 --> 00:04:47,863
Germ-free rats in the 1950s ran into the same problem.

86
00:04:47,863 --> 00:04:52,834
They developed vitamin K deficiency unless it was added to their food.

87
00:04:52,834 --> 00:04:57,024
So in your sterile room, the menu now needs extra vitamins.

88
00:04:57,867 --> 00:05:00,442
Then comes the strangest change of all.

89
00:05:00,442 --> 00:05:03,413
Mice raised germ-free grow an enormous cecum.

90
00:05:03,413 --> 00:05:06,781
That's a pouch at the start of the large intestine.

91
00:05:06,781 --> 00:05:10,215
In mice, it's where a lot of the fermenting happens.

92
00:05:10,715 --> 00:05:13,851
With no microbes to break things down, mucus,

93
00:05:13,851 --> 00:05:17,057
digestive enzymes and water pile up inside it.

94
00:05:17,057 --> 00:05:19,427
In some strains of germ-free mice,

95
00:05:19,427 --> 00:05:24,236
the cecum can reach up to a fifth of the animal's entire body weight.

96
00:05:24,736 --> 00:05:27,909
Nobody knows whether a human gut would do the same.

97
00:05:27,909 --> 00:05:32,015
But if yours did, that pouch would weigh about fourteen kilograms.

98
00:05:32,015 --> 00:05:33,073
Roughly a beagle.

99
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:37,519
Quick favor before we go on:

100
00:05:37,519 --> 00:05:40,410
if you're enjoying this, tap like and subscribe.

101
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:42,096
It really helps the channel.

102
00:05:42,596 --> 00:05:47,513
Over the following weeks, your immune system goes through a slow, quiet change.

103
00:05:47,513 --> 00:05:49,816
A big part of it sits along your gut,

104
00:05:49,816 --> 00:05:52,928
and it learns by constantly meeting your bacteria.

105
00:05:53,428 --> 00:05:58,738
Animals raised germ-free tend to have underdeveloped immune tissue in the gut,

106
00:05:58,738 --> 00:06:01,189
fewer of some kinds of immune cells,

107
00:06:01,189 --> 00:06:03,775
and less of a gut antibody called IgA.

108
00:06:04,275 --> 00:06:06,773
Your bacteria also work like bouncers.

109
00:06:06,773 --> 00:06:11,703
They take up the space, and eat the food, that a dangerous germ would need.

110
00:06:11,703 --> 00:06:14,661
Scientists call this colonization resistance.

111
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:18,589
And we know what happens when the bouncers leave.

112
00:06:18,589 --> 00:06:21,804
Antibiotics can wipe out many gut bacteria at once.

113
00:06:21,804 --> 00:06:23,569
That's when a germ called C.

114
00:06:23,569 --> 00:06:24,767
diff can take over.

115
00:06:25,267 --> 00:06:33,408
One CDC estimate counted more than four hundred and fifty thousand of these infections in the United States in a single year,

116
00:06:33,408 --> 00:06:36,274
linked to about twenty-nine thousand deaths.

117
00:06:36,774 --> 00:06:39,682
In your sterile room, there's nothing to catch.

118
00:06:39,682 --> 00:06:44,198
But your gut is now like a house with nobody home and the doors unlocked.

119
00:06:45,033 --> 00:06:47,621
Germ-free mice even behave differently.

120
00:06:47,621 --> 00:06:49,281
In a 2004 study in Japan,

121
00:06:49,281 --> 00:06:55,253
they released far more stress hormones than normal mice when they were briefly restrained.

122
00:06:55,753 --> 00:06:59,881
Yet in a 2011 study in Sweden, germ-free mice were bolder.

123
00:06:59,881 --> 00:07:03,439
They explored more, and spent more time in bright,

124
00:07:03,439 --> 00:07:06,001
open spaces that mice usually avoid.

125
00:07:06,501 --> 00:07:10,287
Giving the mice bacteria early in life reversed these effects.

126
00:07:10,287 --> 00:07:14,011
How much of this applies to people is still an open question.

127
00:07:16,767 --> 00:07:19,102
So could you live like this for years?

128
00:07:19,102 --> 00:07:19,839
Animals can.

129
00:07:19,839 --> 00:07:22,728
But it took scientists a long time to find out.

130
00:07:23,228 --> 00:07:24,108
In the 1880s,

131
00:07:24,108 --> 00:07:29,997
Louis Pasteur suspected that animals might not be able to live without bacteria at all.

132
00:07:29,997 --> 00:07:30,877
In the 1890s,

133
00:07:30,877 --> 00:07:35,887
two researchers in Berlin raised germ-free guinea pigs for the first time,

134
00:07:35,887 --> 00:07:37,241
for just a few days.

135
00:07:37,741 --> 00:07:41,186
By the 1940s, labs were raising whole colonies.

136
00:07:41,186 --> 00:07:46,831
Bengt Gustafsson in Sweden bred germ-free rats in steam-sterilized isolators.

137
00:07:46,831 --> 00:07:49,470
And at the University of Notre Dame,

138
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:54,235
a lab called LOBUND kept germ-free animals inside steel chambers.

139
00:07:54,735 --> 00:07:58,172
Today, germ-free mice are a standard research tool.

140
00:07:58,172 --> 00:08:00,598
With a fortified diet, they do fine.

141
00:08:00,598 --> 00:08:05,721
Though in some strains, that giant cecum causes serious trouble as they age.

142
00:08:06,567 --> 00:08:08,851
For humans, sterile rooms are real.

143
00:08:08,851 --> 00:08:11,724
But they're a medical tool, not a lifestyle.

144
00:08:11,724 --> 00:08:19,884
Babies born without a working immune system may need to stay in sterile isolation while they wait for a stem cell transplant.

145
00:08:20,384 --> 00:08:25,243
So a germ-free you could probably survive, with extra vitamins and a lot of care.

146
00:08:25,243 --> 00:08:27,162
But you'd be living in a bubble,

147
00:08:27,162 --> 00:08:30,402
with a big part of your body's machinery switched off.

148
00:08:31,267 --> 00:08:32,524
And here's the twist.

149
00:08:32,524 --> 00:08:35,759
The moment you open that door, the experiment is over.

150
00:08:36,259 --> 00:08:43,817
A Yale study found that a single person's presence can add around thirty-seven million bacteria to a room's air every hour,

151
00:08:43,817 --> 00:08:46,153
much of it stirred up from floor dust.

152
00:08:46,653 --> 00:08:52,457
A single day of meals can carry anywhere from about a million to over a billion live microbes,

153
00:08:52,457 --> 00:08:53,816
depending on the diet.

154
00:08:54,316 --> 00:08:56,805
Every breath, every handshake, every bite.

155
00:08:56,805 --> 00:08:59,592
You'd start picking up new bacteria right away.

156
00:09:00,433 --> 00:09:03,137
And a gut can rebuild surprisingly fast.

157
00:09:03,137 --> 00:09:08,408
In a 2018 study, twelve healthy men took a strong four-day mix of antibiotics.

158
00:09:08,408 --> 00:09:13,342
Within about six weeks, their gut communities were close to normal again.

159
00:09:13,842 --> 00:09:14,934
But not completely.

160
00:09:14,934 --> 00:09:21,194
Nine species that every one of them carried at the start were still missing in most of them six months later.

161
00:09:22,033 --> 00:09:23,732
And here's the best part.

162
00:09:23,732 --> 00:09:25,770
You've already done this once.

163
00:09:25,770 --> 00:09:26,314
In 2023,

164
00:09:26,314 --> 00:09:32,360
a group of forty-six experts concluded that babies in the womb don't carry live bacteria.

165
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,874
You started life essentially sterile.

166
00:09:35,374 --> 00:09:38,731
Then, during and after birth, you were colonized.

167
00:09:38,731 --> 00:09:44,964
Babies born vaginally first pick up bacteria that resemble their mother's vaginal microbes.

168
00:09:44,964 --> 00:09:48,937
Babies born by C-section pick up more skin bacteria first.

169
00:09:49,437 --> 00:09:53,369
So your thirty-eight trillion passengers aren't a fixed part of you.

170
00:09:53,369 --> 00:09:55,740
They're a crowd that moved in on day one,

171
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:58,342
and they've been coming and going ever since.

172
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:03,421
So if every bacterium in your body vanished, you'd be a little lighter,

173
00:10:03,421 --> 00:10:06,512
a lot less smelly, and missing a huge chemistry set.

174
00:10:06,512 --> 00:10:10,316
And the second you stepped outside, they'd start moving back in.

175
00:10:10,816 --> 00:10:14,767
If you could keep just one of your bacteria's jobs, which one would it be?

176
00:10:14,767 --> 00:10:16,048
Tell me in the comments.
